WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.589
Roll a red die and a blue die

2
00:00:05.589 --> 00:00:07.830
and add the two numbers.

3
00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.622
Here is every roll

4
00:00:09.622 --> 00:00:12.042
the red die picks the row

5
00:00:12.042 --> 00:00:14.821
the blue die picks the column

6
00:00:14.821 --> 00:00:17.420
and each cell shows the sum.

7
00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:20.216
The sum can be two, three

8
00:00:20.216 --> 00:00:22.473
and so on up to twelve.

9
00:00:22.473 --> 00:00:25.296
That is eleven possible sums.

10
00:00:25.296 --> 00:00:26.989
So a friend says

11
00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:29.717
the chance of a sum of seven

12
00:00:29.811 --> 00:00:31.410
is one in eleven.

13
00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.212
Is that right?

14
00:00:33.212 --> 00:00:35.080
Pause and decide.

15
00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:39.866
Count instead.

16
00:00:39.866 --> 00:00:43.571
There are six times six, thirty-six pairs

17
00:00:43.657 --> 00:00:45.897
and for fair dice they are

18
00:00:45.983 --> 00:00:47.620
all equally likely.

19
00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:51.320
A sum of seven fills six cells

20
00:00:51.428 --> 00:00:54.346
the whole diagonal from one

21
00:00:54.454 --> 00:00:56.939
and six to six and one.

22
00:00:56.939 --> 00:01:01.261
So its chance is six out of thirty-six

23
00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:02.450
one sixth.

24
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.806
A sum of twelve needs six and six: one cell

25
00:01:06.806 --> 00:01:08.536
one in thirty-six.

26
00:01:08.536 --> 00:01:12.178
The eleven sums are not equally likely

27
00:01:12.269 --> 00:01:14.546
so one in eleven is wrong

28
00:01:14.637 --> 00:01:16.640
for every one of them.

29
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.192
Count the cells for each sum.

30
00:01:20.192 --> 00:01:22.140
Sum two: one cell.

31
00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.780
Sum three: two.

32
00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:25.112
Four: three.

33
00:01:25.112 --> 00:01:26.855
Then four, five

34
00:01:26.855 --> 00:01:30.340
and six cells for a sum of seven.

35
00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:34.921
After seven the counts go back down, five

36
00:01:34.921 --> 00:01:39.252
four, three, two, and one cell for twelve.

37
00:01:39.252 --> 00:01:41.770
Thirty-six cells in all.

38
00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:46.305
This is a sample space: a pair, S and P.

39
00:01:46.305 --> 00:01:50.655
S is a nonempty set, the possible outcomes

40
00:01:50.655 --> 00:01:52.831
here the eleven sums.

41
00:01:52.831 --> 00:01:56.390
P gives each outcome a probability.

42
00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:57.598
Two rules.

43
00:01:57.598 --> 00:02:00.259
Every P of s is at least zero

44
00:02:00.259 --> 00:02:02.920
and all of them add up to one.

45
00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:06.267
The sum eight gets five thirty-sixths

46
00:02:06.267 --> 00:02:09.700
the height of its bar, over thirty-six.

47
00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.897
When every outcome gets

48
00:02:11.977 --> 00:02:13.664
the same probability

49
00:02:13.664 --> 00:02:15.992
the space is called uniform.

50
00:02:15.992 --> 00:02:18.723
The thirty-six pairs are uniform.

51
00:02:18.723 --> 00:02:20.730
The eleven sums are not.

52
00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:24.039
And here is a P that fails.

53
00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:27.071
Give each face of one die the

54
00:02:27.172 --> 00:02:29.395
probability one fifth.

55
00:02:29.395 --> 00:02:33.640
Six faces make six fifths, more than one.

56
00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.470
That is not a sample space.

57
00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:39.827
Back to the thirty-six pairs

58
00:02:39.827 --> 00:02:43.945
each with probability one over thirty-six.

59
00:02:43.945 --> 00:02:46.914
An event is a set of outcomes.

60
00:02:46.914 --> 00:02:50.170
E is the event: the sum is eight.

61
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:53.601
Its five cells run along a diagonal.

62
00:02:53.601 --> 00:02:56.767
P of E is the sum over its outcomes

63
00:02:56.767 --> 00:02:59.249
five times one thirty-sixth

64
00:02:59.249 --> 00:03:00.960
five thirty-sixths.

65
00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.598
D is the event: a double.

66
00:03:03.598 --> 00:03:06.526
Six cells on the main diagonal

67
00:03:06.526 --> 00:03:08.265
six thirty-sixths.

68
00:03:08.265 --> 00:03:10.736
When the space is uniform

69
00:03:10.736 --> 00:03:14.030
probability is just counting cells.

70
00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.092
Events are sets

71
00:03:16.092 --> 00:03:19.333
so set operations make new events.

72
00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:23.038
E and D, the intersection: both happen.

73
00:03:23.038 --> 00:03:24.890
Only four and four.

74
00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:29.853
E or D, the union: at least one happens.

75
00:03:29.853 --> 00:03:32.966
Five cells plus six cells

76
00:03:32.966 --> 00:03:38.040
but four and four is in both, so ten cells.

77
00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:39.541
E minus D

78
00:03:39.541 --> 00:03:43.569
the sum is eight but not a double.

79
00:03:43.569 --> 00:03:44.950
Four cells.

80
00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:49.524
And D bar, the complement: not a double.

81
00:03:49.524 --> 00:03:53.220
The thirty cells off the diagonal.

82
00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:58.357
Is P of E or D just P of E plus P of D?

83
00:03:58.474 --> 00:04:03.265
Five thirty-sixths plus six thirty-sixths

84
00:04:03.382 --> 00:04:06.187
is eleven thirty-sixths.

85
00:04:06.187 --> 00:04:10.160
But the union has only ten cells.

86
00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:14.519
The cell four and four is in both events

87
00:04:14.519 --> 00:04:17.257
so the sum counts it twice.

88
00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:20.679
Put the overlap on the other side

89
00:04:20.679 --> 00:04:24.492
P of A plus P of B equals P of A or B

90
00:04:24.492 --> 00:04:26.350
plus P of A and B.

91
00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:27.200
Why?

92
00:04:27.257 --> 00:04:30.373
Cut A or B into three pieces

93
00:04:30.484 --> 00:04:32.710
that do not overlap.

94
00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:36.606
Only in A, in both, and only in B.

95
00:04:36.606 --> 00:04:40.613
P of A covers the first two pieces

96
00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:42.950
P of B the last two

97
00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:47.180
so the middle piece is counted twice.

98
00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:50.415
So when A and B do not overlap

99
00:04:50.415 --> 00:04:53.578
like a double and a sum of seven

100
00:04:53.578 --> 00:04:56.370
the probabilities simply add.

101
00:04:56.950 --> 00:05:00.686
Now: what is the chance of at least one six?

102
00:05:00.686 --> 00:05:04.506
The red die shows six with chance one sixth

103
00:05:04.506 --> 00:05:06.034
the blue die too.

104
00:05:06.034 --> 00:05:09.430
So one sixth plus one sixth, one third?

105
00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:11.345
Look at the cells.

106
00:05:11.432 --> 00:05:14.213
The red six row and the blue six

107
00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:17.516
column share the corner, six and six.

108
00:05:17.516 --> 00:05:19.863
One third counts it twice.

109
00:05:19.863 --> 00:05:23.340
The union has eleven cells, not twelve.

110
00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:26.462
A quicker way is the complement.

111
00:05:26.462 --> 00:05:29.408
No six at all is five times five

112
00:05:29.408 --> 00:05:31.054
twenty-five cells.

113
00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:34.519
So at least one six has probability one

114
00:05:34.606 --> 00:05:37.551
minus twenty-five over thirty-six

115
00:05:37.551 --> 00:05:40.410
which is eleven over thirty-six.

116
00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:45.698
In general, P of not A is one minus P of A.

117
00:05:45.813 --> 00:05:48.798
And P of A or B is at most

118
00:05:48.913 --> 00:05:51.095
P of A plus P of B.

119
00:05:51.095 --> 00:05:55.803
That is the union bound: an upper bound

120
00:05:55.803 --> 00:05:57.640
not the answer.

121
00:05:58.220 --> 00:05:59.647
Try it yourself

122
00:05:59.736 --> 00:06:02.769
find the chance that the larger of

123
00:06:02.858 --> 00:06:05.712
the two numbers is exactly four.

124
00:06:05.712 --> 00:06:07.941
Hint: both at most four

125
00:06:07.941 --> 00:06:10.260
minus both at most three.

126
00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:14.002
In part two, we learn something about the

127
00:06:14.085 --> 00:06:15.905
roll before we answer.

128
00:06:15.905 --> 00:06:18.304
Given that the sum is eight

129
00:06:18.304 --> 00:06:20.290
how likely is a double?
